course: dictionary
lesson: 16004
title: Validly: correct by the rules, and sound by the logic
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Validly» comes from «valid», and it tells you how something was done. Ask yourself: was it done properly, the way the rules say? If yes, it was done validly.
explain: «Mia's vote was validly cast, because she marked one box and signed the form.» The clues are the rules being followed. A vote that follows the rules is valid, so validly means in a way that is officially correct.
explain: In maths and logic, the word means the reasons really lead to the answer. «The argument was validly made: all cats are animals, Tom is a cat, so Tom is an animal.» Each step follows from the one before, so the reasoning is sound.
fact: Validly means in a correct, accepted way, following the rules or the law.
fact: In logic, validly means the conclusion really follows from the reasons.
fact: «Validly» is the adverb form of «valid», so it tells you how something is done.
fact: To check a meaning, swap in "correctly, by the rules" or "with sound reasons" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The form was validly signed by both parents.» What does validly mean here? | Twice, one after the other | In pencil, so it can be rubbed out | In a rush, without looking | *In the correct, accepted way, as the rules say
exercise: «The conclusion follows validly from the facts, so the argument holds.» Which phrase means the same as validly here? | With a loud voice | By guessing the answer | With lots of colours | *With sound reasons that really lead to the conclusion
exercise: «Mia's vote was validly cast.» Find and copy the word that tells you how the vote was cast. | *validly | Mia's | vote | was
test: «The judge said the contract was validly made, so it counts.» What does validly mean? | Happily | *Officially correct, as the law accepts | Very quietly | Carelessly and quickly
test: Ella says, «My argument is validly made, because each step leads to the next.» What does Ella mean? | She wrote it in capital letters | Her argument is very short | She has finished the page | *Her reasons really lead to the conclusion
exam: «The scientist measured the plant validly, with a fair test, so the result can be trusted.» What does validly suggest? | Done in a hurry with no notes | Done by guessing | Done without any numbers | *Done properly, by a fair and correct method
